# Printed once, on a fresh install only.
#
# This package deliberately ships no systemd preset and enables nothing: installing a
# program that can suspend a machine is not the same act as permitting it to. That
# design is only defensible if the package says how to grant the permission, otherwise
# it installs and appears to do nothing at all.

post_install() {
	cat <<'EOF'

idlectl is installed and nothing is enabled yet, on purpose.

  sudo systemctl enable --now idlepolicyd.service    # the decider
  systemctl --user enable --now idlectl-agent.service  # per-session idle and media

The vendor policy at /usr/lib/idlectl/idlectl.toml is already in effect once the daemon
starts; /etc/idlectl/ is yours and this package never writes there.

  idlectl doctor      # what it can see, and who else owns power on this machine

Two things that surprise people, both documented in the README:

  * The agent is PER SESSION and is not enabled by any preset. Without it the daemon
    holds every sleep action, on purpose: a compositor is running, so somebody could
    be sitting in front of it and nothing is in a position to say otherwise.
  * Asking remotely needs a polkit rule. The shipped default requires authentication
    from anywhere that is not the seat, and every ssh session is "anywhere else", so
    a relay gets AccessDenied until you grant it .rest -- and never .rest-forced.

EOF
}
